PR #104  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Without Svukte, U-mode access to S-mode memory may raise faults in address-dependent time, leaking supervisor address space layout through a timing side channel
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/sbi.h:15-49  enum sbi_ext_id
      SBI_EXT_SSE (0x535345) is absent from the canonical extension-ID enumeration. All standard SBI v3.0+ extensions (FWFT, MPXY, DBTR) are listed, but the SSE extension ID—required for firmware-injected event delivery including LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI and GLOBAL_UNKNOWN_NMI—is missing. Without this enumeration entry, no downstack code can probe, register, or receive SSE events. The implicit invariant 'firmware will never inject an SSE event that the kernel must handle' is not enforced anywhere; if firmware does inject SSE events, the kernel has no ID to match against.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/sbi.c:543-553  sbi_probe_extension
      sbi_probe_extension() is a generic ecall(SBI_EXT_BASE, SBI_EXT_BASE_PROBE_EXT, extid) gateway with correct error handling (returns 0 on error, ret.value on success). The function itself has no filtering, no hardcoded allowlist, and no coupling to any specific extension. If SBI_EXT_SSE were added to the enum, this function would probe it correctly without modification. The 'absence' of SSE probing is a caller-side issue in sbi_init(), not a defect in this gateway.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/sbi.c:649-709  sbi_init
      sbi_init() probes TIME, IPI, RFENCE, SRST, DBCN, FWFT, and MPXY at boot, but has no SBI_EXT_SSE probe. This is the concrete integration gap: without probing SSE during init, the kernel can never discover firmware SSE capability, can never register an SSE event handler, and can never receive firmware-injected events (LOCAL_UNKNOWN_NMI, GLOBAL_UNKNOWN_NMI). The implicit assumption 'firmware either doesn't support SSE or will never inject events that the kernel needs to receive' is unverified and unenforced. If firmware silently supports SSE and injects events, those events are lost with no error indication.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:187-187  handle_exception
      csrr s4, CSR_CAUSE reads the raw hardware trap cause into s4. This is the correct and necessary entry-point operation—the kernel must read CSR_CAUSE to dispatch. The csrr instruction itself has constant latency (no page-table walk, no TLB interaction). No timing side channel exists at this single instruction. The issue is not with the read but with what happens downstream when the cause code is unrecognized (handled at line 218-237).
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:218-237  handle_exception
      Exception dispatch is correctly implemented: bge s4,zero branches interrupts (MSB=1) to do_irq; exceptions index into excp_vect_table with a bounds check (bgeu t0,t2,3f) that falls through to do_trap_unknown for out-of-range codes. The code is defensively correct—neither missing nor misplaced branches. The fact that cause codes 16-17 (which firmware should have intercepted via SSE) land in do_trap_unknown is a table-configuration decision at excp_vect_table, not a dispatch logic bug.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:312-316  ret_from_exception
      ret_from_exception executes mret (M-mode) or sret (S-mode) to return from exception handling. This is the architecturally correct exception-return instruction. The sret/mret boundary is indeed where asynchronous SSE event injection from firmware can occur, but the return instruction itself is not buggy—it correctly restores the prior privilege mode and pc. The absence of an SSE handler registered to receive events at this boundary is a system-integration gap (no SSE probe at init), not a flaw in the return path.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:498-499  excp_vect_table
      Exception causes 16 and 17 are statically mapped to do_trap_unknown. These are reserved/undefined hardware cause codes per the RISC-V privileged spec. The table configuration is technically correct for the current spec. However, the implicit assumption is 'no future hardware or firmware will assign meaning to cause codes 16-17 that requires differentiated handling.' If firmware were to inject classified NMI events through hardware cause codes 16-17 (as an SSE-alternative path), the kernel would lose all semantic differentiation by routing both to the same do_trap_unknown handler. This is a forward-compatibility risk, not a current-spec violation.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:166-167  do_trap_unknown
      do_trap_unknown is expanded via DO_ERROR_INFO(SIGILL, ILL_ILLTRP, "unknown exception"). For user-mode traps, this sends SIGILL to the faulting process—the architecturally correct response to an unrecognized exception cause. For kernel-mode traps, it enters NMI context and calls do_trap_error(). The handler correctly distinguishes user vs kernel context. The handler does not itself create a timing side channel; its constant execution path (signal delivery or kernel oops) does not vary based on the trapped address. The upstream issue—that firmware-classified events arrive here as raw unknown traps—is an integration gap, not a bug in this handler.
